// Seller Home — Skills library
// =====================================================================
// Data source for /skills.html. Read at build time by
// gulpfile.js:getData() because the filename matches the .html stem.
//
// ── The Skill / RecentChat split ──────────────────────────────────
// A Skill is a REUSABLE CAPABILITY. It is a static catalog entry —
// nothing on a Skill describes how any particular run is going. Run-
// time state (running / completed / failed, in-flight progress, live
// aria-labels for a progress ring) belongs to `RecentChat` — see
// `recent_chats.json` for that data model and its own runStatuses
// registry.
//
// The rule the codebase follows, everywhere:
//
//     Do NOT derive a Skill card's visual state from an active run.
//
// SkillCard always renders the same leading indicator (an "add"
// glyph inside a neutral outline circle), regardless of whether an
// execution of this skill is currently in flight somewhere in the
// system. If a RecentChat happens to be running THIS skill, its
// progress ring surfaces on the RecentChat row — not on the Skill.
//
// This split lets the Skills page read as a stable, browsable
// catalog of things Sales Agent can do, while activity monitoring
// lives on a separate surface tuned for that job. The two data
// models never share a field — no `runtimeState`, no `progress`,
// no `runStatus` on a Skill; conversely no `ownership`, no
// `connectedTools`, no `canEdit` on a RecentChat.
//
// ── Data-model contract ───────────────────────────────────────────
// Every top-level key documented below is part of the Skills page's
// public data contract. Renderers (template + JS) reference these
// keys by name; a rename requires a matching template edit. Skill
// cards are NEVER hardcoded in the markup — the entire grid is
// projected from `skills[]` through the shared macros in
// `_skill_card_grid.html`.
//
// Fields on each `skills[]` entry (schema — order below matches
// the render order in the card macro so authors can reason about
// what shows up on the card by scrolling top-to-bottom):
//
//   id             — string. Stable DOM + event key. Kebab-cased,
//                     prefixed `sk-` so it's easy to grep for.
//                     Preserved across renames so a route like
//                     `/skills/:id` never breaks when the display
//                     name changes.
//   name           — string. Semibold card title.
//   agent          — string, optional. A KEY into `agentLibrary`
//                     (see below) naming the agent that owns this
//                     skill. Renders as a tinted pill immediately to
//                     the right of the title in TWO places: the list
//                     row (Figma "Source Tile", node 9723:17395 →
//                     11020:23685) and the skill-detail header, next
//                     to the breadcrumb switcher (Figma 9923:31084).
//                     The list resolves the label in the template;
//                     the detail page resolves it in skill_detail.js,
//                     because `?id=` is what decides the owner there.
//                     Purely additive: omit the key and both surfaces
//                     render a bare title — each gates the whole pill
//                     on the lookup resolving, so a missing key and a
//                     typo'd one both degrade to no badge rather than
//                     an empty pill. Assistive tech reaches the label
//                     differently per surface: the row folds it into
//                     the anchor's aria-label ("Open skill, {name},
//                     {agent label}"), since an anchor's label
//                     overrides its inner text; the detail header has
//                     no anchor, so the pill carries a visually-
//                     hidden "Owned by" prefix instead. One agent per
//                     skill — the design shows a single badge, and the
//                     pill does not wrap or truncate.
//   status         — string, optional. A KEY into `statusLibrary`
//                     (see below) naming where the skill sits in its
//                     publishing lifecycle. Renders as a second pill
//                     right of the agent badge, in the skill-detail
//                     header ONLY (Figma 9744:24364) — the list rows
//                     deliberately don't repeat it, since a catalog of
//                     published skills would show the same pill on
//                     every row. Resolves and degrades exactly like
//                     `agent`: an unknown key renders no badge.
//   description    — string. 12-px muted body copy; the card
//                     applies a 2-line clamp with ellipsis so long
//                     descriptions truncate visibly rather than
//                     wrapping unbounded.
//   category       — "mine" | "shared" | "learned". Presentation
//                     bucket the skill belongs to. Preferred field
//                     name going forward; the legacy `ownership`
//                     field below MIRRORS this value 1:1 until every
//                     downstream consumer (tab-count SSR loop in
//                     `skills.html`, `data-sh-skill-ownership`
//                     attribute in `_skill_list.html`, and
//                     `home.js`'s ownership filter) is migrated
//                     to `category`. Dual-writing today keeps the
//                     schema forward-compatible without a
//                     synchronized template + JS + JSON edit; a
//                     future cleanup can drop `ownership` in one
//                     pass. Kept as a required string so the tab
//                     count is always deterministic.
//   ownership      — "mine" | "shared" | "learned". LEGACY mirror
//                     of `category` (see above). Retained because
//                     the tab-count SSR loop in `skills.html` and
//                     the `data-sh-skill-ownership` filter
//                     attribute both read this key today. Adding a
//                     new bucket is a two-line change: append a
//                     value here + `category` above, and add a
//                     matching tab entry in `tabs.items` with its
//                     `ownerships` list.
//   connectedTools — array<string>. Each entry is a KEY into
//                     `toolLibrary` (see below). Rendered as small
//                     icon chips beneath the description in the
//                     order authored. Missing keys are silently
//                     skipped so a typo can't break the whole card.
//                     Kept as key references (not embedded
//                     objects) so the icon assets are swappable
//                     without touching skills[].
//   nextRun        — string | null. Display value for a future
//                     "Next run …" affordance. Not rendered on the
//                     card today (the design ships without it);
//                     kept in the data model so a future revision
//                     can pick it up without a schema migration.
//                     Reserved for RecentChat- or Schedule-shaped
//                     surfaces, not the Skill card — the Skill /
//                     RecentChat split (see below) rules run-time
//                     metadata off Skill cards.
//   sharedBy       — string | null. Person or team who shared a
//                     `ownership: "shared"` skill. Rendered on
//                     detail views (not on the grid card).
//   canEdit        — boolean. Whether the current user may edit
//                     this skill. Consumed by the overflow menu
//                     to gate Edit / Schedule / Duplicate.
//   canShare       — boolean. Whether the user may share it.
//                     Consumed by the overflow menu (Share).
//   canDelete      — boolean. Whether the user may delete it.
//                     Consumed by the overflow menu (Delete).
//   actions        — array<string>. Denormalized list of action
//                     ids the CURRENT USER can invoke on this
//                     skill, derived from the permission booleans
//                     above cross-referenced against
//                     `rowActions[].requires`. Kept as an explicit
//                     scalar so detail views + future surfaces can
//                     render an "Actions on this skill" cluster
//                     without repeating the permission-filter
//                     computation the overflow-menu macro already
//                     does. Order matches the authored order in
//                     `rowActions[]` so a rendering that surfaces
//                     these directly preserves the intended
//                     display order without a sort step. Rebuilt
//                     whenever the permission booleans change.
//   linked         — boolean, optional. UI-only flag — `false`
//                     opts the card out of drill-in (renders as
//                     an inert `<div>` with no chevron and no
//                     card-activation handler). Every card in this
//                     increment ships `linked: true` per the
//                     SkillCard spec ("Make the complete card
//                     interactive"). Detail view isn't wired yet,
//                     so the click-handler in bindSkills()
//                     preventDefault's `href="#"` and dispatches
//                     `sh:skill-open` as a stand-in until the
//                     detail route lands.
//
// ── Fields DELIBERATELY ABSENT ────────────────────────────────────
// The following fields USED to live on `skills[i]` and were removed
// on 2026-08-03 as part of the Skill / RecentChat split:
//
//   runtimeState   — Was "idle" | "running" | "scheduled" | "paused".
//                     Now conceptually a RecentChat's `runStatus`.
//                     See `recent_chats.json`.
//   progress       — Was 0-100. Now lives on RecentChat only, since
//                     progress is a property of a specific run, not
//                     of the reusable capability.
//   lastRun        — Was a display-ready "Last run …" string
//                     rendered in the card footer's right column.
//                     Removed as a follow-on cleanup: "when did this
//                     capability last execute" is a property of a
//                     specific run, not the capability itself.
//                     Surfaces instead on the running-chat row in
//                     the Recents nav (see `renderRunningRing` in
//                     `_home_left_nav.html`); a future "completed
//                     X ago" affordance on a completed RecentChat
//                     is where a matching non-running variant will
//                     land.
//
// The paired `runtimeStates` registry that used to sit at the top
// level of this file (mapping state keys to indicator variant /
// badge / progress-ring aria) was likewise moved — see
// `recent_chats.json`'s `runStatuses` registry.
//
// If you find yourself wanting to add a run-flavored field to a
// Skill entry, that's the signal to add it to RecentChat instead.
//
// Field-by-field for the top-level registries:
//
//   • toolLibrary   — keyed registry of connector-tool icons.
//                      Fields:
//                        name      — display name (SR + tooltip)
//                        iconRef   — path to the 32×32 icon asset
//                                    in /assets/images/skill-tools/
//                      Cards reference tools by key
//                      (`skills[i].connectedTools`). Renaming an
//                      icon or swapping its SVG is a one-line edit
//                      here; cards don't need to know the path.
//                      Filenames use the `tools_*` prefix that
//                      matches the delivered asset set.
//
// Other top-level keys (comments live inline where useful):
//   pageTitle, header, composer, tabs, rowActions, filterAction,
//   emptyState.
//
// TWO URLs project from this one file: /skills.html (Version A) and
// /skills-v2.html (Version B), added 2026-08-17 to review two card
// designs side by side. The v2 stem does NOT match this filename, so
// that page reads this file explicitly with `readData("skills")` — see
// `_skills_body_v1.html`. Consequence worth knowing before you rename
// a key: neither URL holds its own copy of any skill, label, or count.
// `prototypeVersions` (the switcher) exists only for that comparison and
// is meant to be deleted once a direction is chosen.
{
  "pageTitle": "Skills",

  // Header band on /skills.html.
  //   heading     — <h1> at the left of the header band.
  //   actionLabel — Label + accessible name of the right-anchored
  //                 "Create Skill" pill (Figma "Coworker – Skills").
  //                 Rendered by `skills.html` using the same SLDS 2
  //                 `slds-button_neutral` shape `/knowledge.html`'s
  //                 "New Knowledge" button uses, so height, radius,
  //                 padding, colors, and states come from SLDS
  //                 tokens without a bespoke button rule.
  //
  // The prior `description` string powered the intro paragraph that
  // sat between the header band and the composer. That paragraph
  // was removed on 2026-08-11 when the Skills page adopted the
  // list + banner shape; the string is intentionally dropped to
  // avoid a dormant field on the schema (the educational banner
  // below now carries the equivalent copy).
  // ── Prototype-only design-version switcher ───────────────────
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // Feeds `_prototype_version_switcher.html`, the bottom-right
  // control that hops between the two design versions of this
  // landing page. Strip this block and the partial together when the
  // design question is settled — nothing else reads it, and the
  // switcher hides itself if fewer than two entries survive.
  //
  //   label — Visible chip-group text. Purely a caption; the
  //           control is a <nav> with its own aria-label, so this
  //           string is aria-hidden in the partial.
  //   items — Authored order; the chips render left to right as
  //           listed.
  //     label — Kept to "Version A" / "Version B" rather than the
  //             URL's v1 / v2 because the switcher is read by design
  //             reviewers, not by whoever typed the route. A → v1
  //             and B → v2, deliberately.
  //     href  — Absolute path. Authored here so the two versions can
  //             never disagree about where the other one lives: both
  //             pages render the same list, and each marks the entry
  //             matching its own `skillsVersion` as current. Version
  //             A points at /skills.html — the left nav's own
  //             destination — so there is exactly one URL per design
  //             rather than an alias route.
  //     id    — Matches the `skillsVersion` string the page templates
  //             set (`v1` / `v2`).
  "prototypeVersions": {
    "label": "Design",
    "items": [
      { "id": "v1", "label": "Version A", "href": "/skills.html" },
      { "id": "v2", "label": "Version B", "href": "/skills-v2.html" }
    ]
  },

  // Version B's card body carries NO data of its own. The 2026-08-18
  // pass on Figma 11194:24307 settled it as a restack of facts Version A
  // already renders — the skill name, its agent, and its
  // connected-skills count — so the `rowMeta` block that fed the earlier
  // object tile, object stamp, and "From … • Edited …" footer is gone
  // rather than left dormant. Author and last-edit stay on
  // /skill_detail.html, which reads each skill's own `createdBy` /
  // `updatedAt` directly.
  //
  // ── "Use Skill" button (Version B rows only) ──────────────────
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // The primary half of the card's action cluster (Figma 11194:24333);
  // the bordered kebab beside it is the same overflow menu Version A
  // ships, so it needs no copy of its own. Version A ignores this block
  // entirely, and dropping it degrades Version B's cluster to just the
  // kebab rather than breaking the row.
  //
  //   id            — Action id emitted as `data-sh-skill-action`, which
  //                   routes the click through the SAME
  //                   `sh:skill-action` path the overflow items use.
  //                   `use_in_chat` deliberately matches the verb the
  //                   Skill detail page's own "Use in Chat" button
  //                   fires, so both surfaces mean one thing.
  //   label         — Screen-reader label. Not painted since Figma
  //                   11314:170930 turned this into an icon-only
  //                   button; the detail page's equivalent still says
  //                   "Use in Chat" in text, where there is room.
  //   icon          — SLDS utility sprite id for the glyph, authored
  //                   here for the same reason the overflow items carry
  //                   theirs in data rather than in the template.
  //   assistiveText — Accessible name + tooltip. `{name}` is replaced
  //                   with the skill's name at render time, so the
  //                   button reads uniquely per row instead of nine
  //                   identical "Use Skill" targets.
  "useAction": {
    "id":            "use_in_chat",
    "label":         "Use Skill",
    "icon":          "anywhere_chat",
    "assistiveText": "Use {name} in a new conversation"
  },

  "header": {
    "heading":     "Skills",
    "actionLabel": "Create Skill"
  },

  // ── Educational banner (Figma "Turn your regular tasks into
  //     reusable skills")
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // Renders as a brand-blue hero band beneath the header. Copy is
  // authored here so translators / product edits stay in JSON;
  // markup lives in `_skills_banner.html`.
  //
  //   title       — SLDS Titles/font-scale-3 semibold, white.
  //   description — SLDS Body/font-scale-1 regular, white at ~85%
  //                 alpha (matches the muted-on-accent treatment
  //                 SLDS uses on filled surfaces).
  //   imageSrc    — Path to the mascot cutout (SVG or PNG). Kept
  //                 optional so the banner still renders a clean
  //                 text-only surface when the asset is missing —
  //                 useful during design iteration before the
  //                 final artwork lands.
  //   imageAlt    — Empty string keeps the mascot decorative
  //                 (aria-hidden="true" in the template); the
  //                 banner's aria-labelledby → heading gives
  //                 assistive tech the region label.
  "banner": {
    "title":       "Turn your regular tasks into reusable skills",
    "description": "Let Agentforce Coworker take on the work, like meeting prep or project updates. Create skills for yourself or the whole team.",
    // Full-width banner artwork — brand-blue gradient with the
    // Agentforce mascot + sparkles baked in on the right. Applied
    // as a `background-image` on the banner surface rather than an
    // `<img>` element so the mascot can hide behind the title
    // gracefully at narrow widths without a JS-driven visibility
    // toggle. `imageAlt` stays empty because the banner heading
    // (`aria-labelledby` in the partial) is the accessible name;
    // the mascot itself is decorative.
    "imageSrc":    "/assets/images/skills-banner.jpg",
    "imageAlt":    ""
  },

  // Composer config — DORMANT after 2026-08-11. The composer include
  // was removed from `skills.html` when the page adopted the list +
  // banner shape (Skills is a browsable catalog now, not a "start a
  // chat" surface). Kept here as-is so re-adding the composer is a
  // one-line template edit (`{% include "_home_composer.html" %}`)
  // without a JSON migration. Home / Scheduled continue to use
  // this same shape.
  "composer": {
    "placeholder":   "Start a skill",
    "ariaLabel":     "Start a skill",
    "samplePrompts": [],
    "actions": {
      "start": [
        { "id": "add",          "label": "Add",              "icon": "add" },
        { "id": "quick_action", "label": "Quick action",     "icon": "fallback" },
        { "id": "skills",       "label": "Skills and tools", "icon": "apps" }
      ],
      "end": [
        { "id": "voice",        "label": "Voice input",      "icon": "unmuted" }
      ]
    }
  },

  // First-paint active tab. `my_skills` is the leftmost visible
  // tab after "All" was hidden via `hidden: true` below; when
  // "All" comes back, flip its `hidden` flag and (optionally)
  // change `defaultTabId` back to `"all"`. Any id below is valid;
  // the tab controller falls back to the first authored item if
  // this key ever names an unknown / hidden id.
  "tabs": {
    "defaultTabId": "my_skills",
    "items": [
      // "All" is parked with `hidden: true` — the template skips
      // rendering the button but the entry stays in the authored
      // order so re-enabling is a one-line JSON flip. `bindSkills`
      // never sees a DOM node for a hidden tab, so URL hashes
      // pointing at `#tab=all` gracefully fall back to
      // `defaultTabId`. Kept as a dormant schema slot rather than
      // deleted so we don't lose the intent.
      { "id": "all",             "label": "All",             "ownerships": [],         "hidden": true },
      { "id": "my_skills",       "label": "Your Skills",     "ownerships": ["mine"]    },
      // `learned_skills` is parked with `hidden: true` while the
      // learned-skills surface design is still being iterated on.
      // The three seeded `ownership: "learned"` skills (Review
      // Deal Health, Identify Pipeline Changes, Recommend Next
      // Actions) stay reachable via direct URL and via the
      // supporting-skill chips on Pipeline Snapshot's detail
      // page — they just don't appear under any visible tab while
      // this flag is on. Flip `hidden` to `false` to re-expose the
      // tab, no JS change required.
      { "id": "learned_skills",  "label": "Learned Skills",  "ownerships": ["learned"], "hidden": true },
      { "id": "shared_with_me",  "label": "Shared with you", "ownerships": ["shared"]  }
    ]
  },

  // ── Keyed agent registry ──────────────────────────────────────
  // The agent that OWNS a skill. Skills reference an entry by key via
  // `skills[i].agent`; `label` renders in a pill next to the skill
  // name on the list row (Figma "Source Tile" → badge 11020:23685 for
  // Sales, 11020:23693 for CoWorker) and again in the skill-detail
  // header (Figma 9923:31084).
  //
  // Both surfaces read THIS object: the list macro is handed it as a
  // parameter, and skill_detail.html inlines it into the page config
  // blob for skill_detail.js. Neither keeps its own copy of a label.
  //
  // The KEY doubles as the visual variant: both surfaces emit
  // `.seller-home__skill-agent-badge--<key>`, and home.scss gives
  // each key its own tint so agents stay visually distinguishable at
  // a glance. Adding an agent is therefore a two-step change — an
  // entry here plus a matching variant block in home.scss. Skip the
  // CSS and the pill still renders correctly in a neutral tint (the
  // base rule owns a fallback), so a new agent can never ship an
  // unreadable badge.
  //
  // Labels live here rather than on each skill so the string is
  // authored once; renaming an agent is a one-line edit even when
  // dozens of skills point at it.
  //
  // The labels deliberately OMIT the word "Agent" (directed in review
  // on 2026-08-14): the pill's job is to say which agent, and "Agent"
  // repeated on every row is the part that carries no information. Do
  // not add it back to make the strings read like full product names —
  // prose elsewhere still says "Sales Agent", and that is fine, but the
  // badge is a label, not a sentence.
  "agentLibrary": {
    "sales":    { "label": "Hunter"   },
    "coworker": { "label": "CoWorker" },
    "tableau":  { "label": "Tableau"  }
  },

  // ── Keyed lifecycle-status registry ───────────────────────────
  // Where a skill sits in its publishing lifecycle. Skills reference
  // an entry by key via `skills[i].status`, and `label` renders in a
  // second pill immediately right of the agent badge in the
  // skill-detail header (Figma 9744:24364 → badge 11054:23957).
  //
  // Same registry shape and same resolution rules as `agentLibrary`
  // above — key doubles as the CSS variant
  // (`.seller-home__skill-status-badge--<key>`), the label is
  // authored once, and a key that resolves to nothing renders no
  // badge at all.
  //
  // ONE entry today, which is the point: the badge says "Published"
  // because that is a STATE, and a state implies the others. When
  // Draft / Archived / Needs review arrive, they are an entry here
  // plus a variant block in home.scss — the template and
  // skill_detail.js need no change. Note the tint lives entirely in
  // the variant (the base pill is colorless), so a second status
  // cannot accidentally inherit "published green".
  "statusLibrary": {
    "published": { "label": "Published" }
  },

  // Keyed connector-tool registry. Cards reference by key via
  // `skills[i].connectedTools`. Swap `iconRef` here to replace an
  // icon asset without touching skills[].
  "toolLibrary": {
    "sf":       { "name": "Salesforce",      "iconRef": "/assets/images/skill-tools/tools_sf.png"       },
    "slack":    { "name": "Slack",           "iconRef": "/assets/images/skill-tools/tools_slack.svg"    },
    "gmail":    { "name": "Gmail",           "iconRef": "/assets/images/skill-tools/tools_gmail.png"    },
    "gcal":     { "name": "Google Calendar", "iconRef": "/assets/images/skill-tools/tools_gcal.svg"     },
    "zoominfo": { "name": "ZoomInfo",        "iconRef": "/assets/images/skill-tools/tools_zoominfo.svg" }
  },

  // Six mock skills — order + labels match Figma "Coworker – Skills"
  // (node 9723:17362), 2026-08-11 refactor.
  //
  // Each `ownership: "mine"` entry names its owning agent via
  // `agent` (a key into `agentLibrary` above): the first five belong
  // to `sales`, and Make Powerpoint belongs to `coworker`. The mix is
  // deliberate — this surface lists skills owned by DIFFERENT agents,
  // and the badge tint is what tells them apart.
  //
  // The three `ownership: "learned"` entries below deliberately carry
  // no `agent`: they are sub-skills of Pipeline Snapshot rather
  // than agent-level capabilities, and their tab is parked behind
  // `hidden: true`. Give them one here if the Learned Skills tab is
  // ever re-exposed and the design calls for it — no template or CSS
  // change is needed.
  //
  // Field order below matches the schema docblock at the top of
  // this file so authors can eyeball each entry against the
  // documented contract. IDs are stable — treat them as
  // permanent, since a future `/skills/:id` detail route depends
  // on them.
  //
  // Every field on the entry maps directly to a rendered surface
  // via the row macro OR a downstream detail view; run-flavored
  // fields (runtimeState, progress, lastRun) are DELIBERATELY
  // absent — see the note at the top of this file — those live
  // on RecentChat.
  //
  // ── Denormalized fields (audit note, 2026-08-11) ───────────────
  // `actions` is denormalized: it could be derived at render time
  // from the permission booleans. It's carried explicitly so
  // consumers other than the list row macro don't have to re-run
  // the same computation. Whenever a permission flips, update the
  // list in the same commit — the schema docblock above spells out
  // the invariant.
  "skills": [
    {
      "id":                   "sk-find-prospects",
      "name":                 "Find Prospects",
      "agent":                "sales",
      "status":               "published",
      "description":          "Build deduplicated, prioritized prospect lists by discovering net-new target accounts, identifying relevant people at known accounts, or both.",
      "category":             "mine",
      "ownership":            "mine",
      "connectedTools":       ["sf", "slack", "gmail"],
      "nextRun":              null,
      "sharedBy":             null,
      "canEdit":              true,
      "canShare":             true,
      "canDelete":            true,
      "actions":              ["use_in_chat", "schedule", "duplicate", "share", "delete"],
      "createdBy":            "Caroline Alix",
      "createdAt":            "Jan 12",
      "updatedBy":            "Caroline Alix",
      "updatedAt":            "3 days ago",
      "version":              "V4",
      "markdown":             "# Find Prospects\n\nBuild deduplicated, prioritized prospect lists by discovering net-new target accounts, identifying relevant people at known accounts, or doing both. Use for territory building, account discovery, persona-aligned contact discovery, buying-committee coverage, lookalike prospecting, or list refreshes; do not use for deep account research, outreach drafting or sending, meeting preparation, or pipeline management.\n\n## Purpose\n\nProduce a reviewable list of accounts and/or people who match the organization's target criteria, show relevant timing, and are not hidden duplicates or conflicts in CRM.\n\nUse three modes:\n\n- Account discovery: find and rank net-new target accounts.\n- Contact discovery: find and rank relevant people at specified or CRM accounts.\n- Account + contact discovery: find qualified accounts, then identify the right people within them.\n\nTreat the GTM Profile as the definition of organization-level fit, signals, anti-ICP classes, and personas. Treat CRM and external sources as evidence about each candidate. Named-account or contact restrictions, ownership, customer status, do-not-contact state, and collision rules belong in CRM configuration or policy. Never bake customer-specific criteria, titles, signals, vendor preferences, or suppression rules into this skill.\n\n## Trigger Logic\n\nRun this skill when the user asks to:\n\n- Find companies, accounts, leads, buyers, decision-makers, champions, or other prospects.\n- Build or refresh a territory, target-account list, contact list, or buying committee.\n- Find people with a particular responsibility at one or more known accounts.\n- Find accounts similar to a supplied customer or seed account.\n- Surface net-new prospects showing a relevant buying signal.\n\nInfer mode from the request:\n\n- \"Find companies/accounts\" -> Account discovery.\n- \"Find people/contacts at these accounts\" -> Contact discovery.\n- \"Build a prospect list\" with no object specified -> ask whether the user wants accounts, people at known accounts, or both.\n\nAsk one focused question only when the mode is ambiguous or when a missing territory, target cohort, or required delivery field would materially change the list. Mode is blocking because it changes cost, scope, and output.\n\n## Boundaries and Handoffs\n\nFor every handoff, pass the target record identifiers, bounded goal, user-selected or approved state, evidence references with dates, relevant constraints, and requested return format. Return control to the originating workflow when the handed-off result is needed to finish the request.\n\nFor net-new outbound, use this order: [Find Prospects](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-find-prospects) identifies and verifies candidates; [Account Research](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-account-research-summary) deepens evidence only for user-selected accounts when needed; [Write Sales Emails](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-write-sales-emails) drafts only after the recipient is selected.\n\n- Use [Account Research](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-account-research-summary) for a deep, evidence-backed brief on shortlisted accounts.\n- Use [Write Sales Emails](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-write-sales-emails) after the user selects prospects and requests outreach.\n- Use [Pre-Meeting Prep](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-prepare-for-meetings) for scheduled customer interactions.\n- Use [Pipeline Management](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-pipeline-management) for live opportunity health and progression.\n- Use [Data Sourcing](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-data-sourcing) to select connected providers, enrichment recipes, waterfalls, validation, and fallbacks. Do not embed those choices here.\n- Do not infer or manufacture email addresses, phone numbers, employment, intent, or account fit.\n- Do not create CRM records, enroll sequences, or send messages unless the user separately requests the action and policy permits it.\n\n## Inputs\n\nResolve these inputs before sourcing.\n\n**Required or inferable**\n\n- Mode: account, contact, or account + contact discovery.\n- Scope: geography, segment, product, territory, named accounts, CRM view, or other target universe. Use GTM Profile defaults when the request omits scope.\n- Desired volume: use customer-configured defaults when available.\n\n**Optional**\n\n- Seed accounts, lookalike examples, explicit inclusions, or exclusions.\n- Required roles, responsibilities, seniority, buying-committee coverage, or persona.\n- Timing-signal requirement and lookback window.\n- Required contact channels or fields.\n- CRM owner, campaign, list, segment, or assignment context.\n- User-supplied candidates to enrich, validate, or deduplicate.\n\nIf neither the user nor Knowledge defines volume, use these defaults:\n\n- Account discovery: 25 accounts.\n- Contact discovery: up to 3 relevant people per account and 50 people total.\n- Account + contact discovery: 15 accounts and up to 2 relevant people per account.\n\nTreat these as output limits, not quotas. Return fewer candidates rather than weaken a hard criterion or fabricate data.\n\n## Knowledge Hydration\n\nLoad only the GTM Profile sections relevant to the selected mode. The platform resolves every link below and injects the section content into the runtime context.\n\n**Always load**\n\n- [GTM Profile -> Profile Scope](#gtm-profile-profile-scope)\n- [GTM Profile -> Target Accounts](#gtm-profile-target-accounts)\n\n**Load for account or account + contact discovery**\n\n- [GTM Profile -> Buying Signals](#gtm-profile-buying-signals)\n\n**Load for contact or account + contact discovery**\n\n- [GTM Profile -> Buyer Personas](#gtm-profile-buyer-personas)\n- [GTM Profile -> Buying Process](#gtm-profile-buying-process)\n\n**Load when relevance or prioritization requires it**\n\n- [GTM Profile -> Company and Offer](#gtm-profile-company-and-offer)\n- [GTM Profile -> Customer Problems and Use Cases](#gtm-profile-customer-problems-and-use-cases)\n- [GTM Profile -> Positioning](#gtm-profile-positioning)\n- [GTM Profile -> Objections](#gtm-profile-objections)\n\nExtract hard criteria, soft indicators, organization-level anti-ICP classes, signal definitions and windows, persona responsibilities, and buying roles. Knowledge guides candidate selection; it does not prove a candidate meets a criterion. Read named-account and contact restrictions, ownership, customer state, and do-not-contact rules from CRM configuration and policy.\n\nIf a required section is missing:\n\n1. Use explicit constraints in the user's request.\n2. Do not invent ICP or persona criteria.\n3. Return a clearly labeled partial result if it can still be useful.\n4. State which Knowledge section the admin should complete.\n\nIf a section is marked stale, superseded, draft, or low-confidence, preserve that status. Do not use uncertain guidance as a hard filter or decisive ranking factor; place affected candidates in Review unless the remaining current evidence supports a decision.\n\n## Source and Tool Contract\n\nRead Data Sourcing; the platform injects its content into the same runtime context. This skill defines who and what to find. Data Sourcing defines which providers and actions to call, waterfall order, validation, freshness, cost limits, and stopping conditions.\n\nUse the Data Gateway only to invoke exposed provider actions. The gateway contains no business logic. Never hard-code provider names or assume that a specific account, contact, email, intent, or enrichment action is connected.\n\n**Source roles**\n\n1. CRM: use first for deduplication, existing relationships, ownership, customer status, active opportunities, prior prospecting, contact restrictions, and suppression.\n2. Connected data providers: use according to Data Sourcing for account discovery, contact discovery, enrichment, verification, and provider-reported signals.\n3. Company first-party sources: use to verify domains, company identity, locations, products, hiring, and named leadership or employees when available.\n4. Authoritative external sources: use for dated events that support a buying signal.\n5. Other web sources: use for discovery or corroboration, not as the sole basis for a material decision when stronger evidence exists.\n\n**Evidence standard**\n\nRetain the following for every candidate:\n\n- Canonical identity: company name and domain; for people, full name, current company, and current title or responsibility.\n- Evidence for every hard-fit criterion and any claimed buying signal.\n- CRM match or an explicit \"No match found,\" plus the matching keys used.\n- Source URL or provider/internal record reference, source type, observed or published date, retrieval date, and confidence.\n- For contact methods, provider verification status and verification date.\n\nNever output a guessed or pattern-generated email address or phone number as verified. Label provider-modeled intent and inferred responsibilities explicitly.\n\n**Freshness defaults**\n\nFollow GTM Profile or Data Sourcing rules when present. Otherwise:\n\n- Employment and title: prefer verification within 30 days.\n- Email or phone validity: prefer verification within 90 days.\n- Time-sensitive buying signals: prefer evidence within 90 days.\n- Firmographics: prefer evidence observed within 180 days.\n\nPreserve older data as stale only when it remains useful. When sources conflict, preserve the conflict, prefer first-party and newer evidence for public facts, and lower confidence rather than selecting the convenient answer.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Set the mode and limit. Infer or confirm account, contact, or account + contact discovery; scope; desired volume; required fields; and as-of time.\n2. Hydrate Knowledge. Extract target-account criteria, exclusions, signals, personas, and buying roles. Read territory, ownership, and suppression behavior from live CRM configuration and the CRM Writeback Policy rather than inventing it here.\n3. Build a selection spec separating hard filters, soft indicators, explicit exclusions and inverse signals, persona responsibilities and buying roles, and required evidence and freshness.\n4. Build the CRM suppression set. Retrieve existing accounts, leads, contacts, customers, active opportunities, ownership, recent touches, campaign/list membership, bounce status, do-not-contact flags, and configured exclusions. Record counts by suppression reason.\n5. Generate candidate accounts. Use Data Sourcing rules to search the allowed universe. For lookalikes, derive the relevant attributes from Knowledge and independently verify that each candidate meets them; similarity alone is not qualification.\n6. Resolve and verify accounts. Confirm the canonical domain, parent/subsidiary relationship, hard-fit criteria, exclusions, and signal evidence. Do not spend on contact enrichment for an account that fails a hard filter.\n7. Generate candidate people. In contact modes, search only retained or supplied accounts. Match responsibilities and buying roles from Buyer Personas, not exact title strings alone. Include multiple roles only when they provide useful buying-committee coverage.\n8. Verify employment and contactability. Confirm current employer and role. Enrich only the contact fields required by the request. Preserve verification dates and return \"Not verified\" instead of guessing.\n9. Deduplicate deterministically. Match accounts by CRM ID when present, then canonical domain and parent/subsidiary. Match people by CRM ID, normalized email, or stable professional profile URL; use full name plus company only as a review candidate, never an automatic merge key.\n10. Apply suppression visibly. Exclude configured conflicts from the main list, but report the candidate and reason in an exclusions summary. If no rule governs an existing CRM relationship, flag it for review rather than silently excluding it.\n11. Rank the retained candidates. Apply customer-defined weighting when present. Otherwise use the default decision logic below.\n12. Check coverage. Confirm the result meets the requested account count, persona mix, buying-role mix, geography, and required field coverage without weakening hard criteria.\n13. Stop deliberately. Stop at the requested limit, configured cost/time ceiling, or when additional providers repeat candidates. Report shortfalls and the binding constraint.\n14. Produce the list. Put qualified candidates first, keep evidence adjacent to claims, and make the next handoff explicit.\n\nParallelize independent candidate searches and enrichments where safe. Resolve entities before deduplication or paid enrichment.\n\n## Decision Logic\n\nApply explicit GTM Profile, CRM configuration, and policy rules before these defaults.\n\n**Candidate status**\n\n- Qualified: all hard filters are supported, no exclusion is present, CRM conflict is cleared, and required identity fields are verified.\n- Review: likely fit, but one non-critical criterion, employment fact, CRM match, or buying-role interpretation remains uncertain.\n- Excluded: a hard exclusion, duplicate, current-customer rule, open-opportunity conflict, ownership rule, contact restriction, or explicit user constraint applies.\n- Insufficient data: a required identity, fit, or contactability field cannot be verified.\n\n**Default ranking**\n\nRank using these dimensions in order unless Knowledge supplies weights:\n\n1. Hard-fit confidence.\n2. Current, specific Knowledge-defined buying signal.\n3. Persona and buying-role alignment.\n4. Clean CRM status and territory ownership.\n5. Evidence and contact-data freshness.\n\nDo not rank a person higher only because of seniority. Prefer the person whose responsibilities and role in the buying process best match the GTM Profile. Use uncovered buying roles as a tie-breaker when building a committee.\n\nDo not weaken a hard filter to hit volume. If the qualified set is too small, report which constraint binds and ask whether the user wants to change it.\n\n## Output Requirements\n\n- State the mode, requested volume, actual volume, scope, as-of time, and Knowledge sections used.\n- Show qualified and review candidates separately from exclusions.\n- Explain why every included account fits and, when applicable, why it is timely.\n- Explain why every included person maps to a hydrated persona or buying role.\n- Show the CRM match, owner, and conflict status for every candidate.\n- Include only verified contact fields requested by the user; label unavailable or stale fields.\n- Cite hard-fit facts, signals, employment, and contact verification with sources and dates.\n- Include a suppression and deduplication summary; never silently drop a sourced candidate.\n- End with a clear handoff, such as Account Research on the top accounts or Write Sales Emails for user-selected contacts.\n\n## Output Format\n\nStart every result with:\n\n```\n# Prospect List: {scope or objective}\n**Mode:** {Account / Contact / Account + Contact}\n**As of:** {date and time}\n**Requested / returned:** {count} / {count}\n**Scope:** {territory, segment, named accounts, or other universe}\n**Coverage:** {required fields and completion rate}\n**Knowledge used:** {linked GTM Profile sections}\n\n### Qualified Accounts\n| Rank | Account | Domain | Fit | Why now | CRM status / owner | Confidence | Evidence |\n|---:|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | {account} | {domain} | {matched criteria} | {signal and date, or \"No current signal required\"} | {status / owner} | {High / Medium / Low} | {[S#]} |\n\n### Qualified People\n| Rank | Person | Current role | Account | Persona / buying role | Why this person | Verified contactability | CRM status | Evidence |\n|---:|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | {name} | {title} | {account} | {mapping} | {responsibility-based rationale} | {requested fields, status, and date} | {status / owner} | {[S#]} |\n\nOmit the accounts or people table when the selected mode does not produce it. In account + contact mode, keep people grouped or sortable by account.\n\n### Review Required\n| Candidate | Open question | Why it matters | Best next verification |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| {candidate} | {missing or conflicting fact} | {decision impact} | {specific action} |\n\n### Exclusions and Dedupe Summary\n- **Sourced candidates:** {count}\n- **Qualified:** {count}\n- **Review:** {count}\n- **Excluded:** {count by duplicate, customer, active opportunity, territory, restriction, hard-fit, stale/unverified, and other}\n\n| Candidate | Disposition | Reason | CRM or evidence reference |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| {candidate} | {Excluded / Merged / Suppressed} | {specific rule} | {[S#] or CRM link} |\n\n### Suggested Handoff\n{One recommended next action and the exact subset it applies to.}\n\n### Sources\n- **[S1]** {source label} - {clickable URL or internal/provider record reference}; {published/observed/verified date}; retrieved {date}; {source type}\n```\n\n## Actions and Writeback\n\n- Keep CRM and sourcing tools read-only by default.\n- Follow CRM Writeback Policy before creating or updating accounts, leads, contacts, list membership, campaigns, ownership, or prospecting status.\n- When the user requests writeback, preview the destination object, dedupe key, exact field values, source, owner, and collision behavior before mutation unless policy explicitly pre-authorizes it.\n- Create no CRM record for a Review or Insufficient data candidate unless the user explicitly accepts the gap.\n- Never overwrite verified contact data with stale data, rep-authored context with enrichment, or another seller's ownership.\n- After any policy-permitted write, return one result per target record with status, record ID, old and new values or action, source, error if any, and whether a failed item is safe to retry. Never retry a succeeded item.\n- Never enroll a sequence, draft an email, or send a message from this skill.\n\n## Quality Gate\n\nDo not deliver until all applicable checks pass:\n\n- [ ] Mode, scope, volume, required fields, and as-of time are explicit.\n- [ ] The relevant GTM Profile sections were loaded, or missing sections are disclosed.\n- [ ] Hard filters, soft indicators, exclusions, signals, and persona responsibilities are separated.\n- [ ] Account identities, canonical domains, and parent/subsidiary relationships are resolved.\n- [ ] CRM dedupe, ownership, customer, opportunity, recent-touch, and contact-restriction checks ran.\n- [ ] Every included candidate maps to hydrated account or persona criteria.\n- [ ] Material claims, signals, employment, and contact verification have sources and dates.\n- [ ] Inferred, modeled, stale, missing, and conflicting data is labeled.\n- [ ] No email address, phone number, employment, or intent was guessed.\n- [ ] Suppressed and merged candidates are counted and explained.\n- [ ] Hard criteria were not relaxed to hit volume.\n- [ ] The output is prioritized, scan-friendly, and ready for the stated handoff.\n- [ ] No CRM record, sequence, draft, or message was created without a separate authorized action.\n\n## Feedback Routing\n\nClassify durable feedback before proposing a change:\n\n- Which account classes, personas, buying roles, signals, or anti-ICP categories should our business care about? Propose an edit to the relevant GTM Profile section in Knowledge.\n- How should prospect discovery, qualification, ranking, deduplication, or coverage work? Propose an edit to this Find Prospects skill.\n- How should providers, waterfalls, enrichment fields, verification, freshness, or fallback work? Propose an edit to Data Sourcing.\n- Which provider actions are available or connected? Route to Data Gateway connector and tool configuration; do not add business logic there.\n- What is true about a specific account or person, or should that named record be restricted? Propose an evidence-backed CRM update under CRM Writeback Policy.\n- How may the agent create or update CRM records? Propose an edit to CRM Writeback Policy.\n- Is this instruction only for the current request? Apply it in-session and do not persist it.\n\nFor a durable change, present the destination, exact proposed change, and expected impact through the product's approval flow. Never hide customer-specific targeting or persona definitions inside this skill.\n",
