      "markdown":             "# Make Powerpoint\n\nBuilds a presentation-ready deck from account context, deal history, and the points you want to land.\n\n## What this skill does\n\nMake Powerpoint assembles a slide deck for a specific audience \u2014 a customer QBR, an internal deal review, a renewal pitch \u2014 by pulling the relevant Salesforce records and shaping them into a narrative. It proposes an outline first, then fills each slide with the supporting numbers and a suggested talk track.\n\n## Instructions\n\n1. Ask for the deck's purpose and audience before generating anything.\n2. Identify the accounts or opportunities in scope from Salesforce.\n3. Propose a slide outline and wait for confirmation before building the full deck.\n4. Open with the single most important takeaway rather than an agenda slide.\n5. Put one idea on each slide; move supporting detail into the speaker notes.\n6. Cite the Salesforce record behind every figure that appears on a slide.\n7. Keep the deck under twelve slides unless a longer format is requested.\n\n## Guardrails\n\n- Never invent metrics, quotes, or customer commitments to fill a slide.\n- Flag any slide where the underlying Salesforce data is stale or incomplete.\n- Leave pricing and contractual terms out unless they are explicitly requested.\n- Respect Salesforce permissions and sharing rules when sourcing content.\n- Present the outline for approval before producing the finished deck.\n\n## Example output\n\nA QBR request comes back as a nine-slide outline \u2014 headline takeaway, adoption trend, open risks, and a closing ask \u2014 with each figure traced to the opportunity or usage record it came from and a short talk track in the notes.\n",
      "linked":               true
    },
